# E99 autonomous research task entrypoint

**Permanent policy:** This is an AI-agent-only mathematical research program.
Canonical promotion requires one independent reviewing AI-agent session, which may be
operated by the project owner while auditing other agents' work. The session must
independently reconstruct the complete proof and adversarially test its bridge and
regressions against the exact SHA. No human mathematical approval or second agent
audit is required. See `docs/REVIEW_POLICY.md`.

Use this page when an authorized research assignment does not already name a
bounded question. An explicit assignment takes precedence over random selection.
The task catalogue is a set of starting points, not a record of novelty, an
exclusive reservation, or a second theorem ledger. A useful result may be a
proof candidate, an exact counterexample to a proposed lemma, an independent
audit, or a clear handoff.

## Current mathematical snapshot

This catalogue was checked against `origin/main` at
`ab6dd3336f9f8d440f2bada76edb32153391cddc`, state version
`2026-10-10.audit.234`. The problem remains open; Level I is open.

- **Target A:** the canonical route runs through Section 177. The global
  closure is `rank S <= d_int + |C^partial| - Delta_Lambda`, equivalently
  `d_sc >= Delta_Lambda`. The locked-response branch has the Section-175
  right-deficiency endpoint; the zero-slack/source branch also remains open.
- **Target B:** the exact sufficient closure is
  `G+E >= (sqrt(3)/4) B + (sqrt(3)/2)(t+chi-1)`. The one-component GPI
  estimate and coherent-core exclusions do not prove the required global
  multi-grain inequality.
- **Target C:** C-I is closed and C-II is open. The current finite-move
  endpoint requires an actual source move or discrete replacement that checks
  every full-source lower constraint and actual-diameter upper constraint,
  then loses a contact at diameter `D` or yields a same-cardinality competitor
  of diameter `< D`. Keep the macroscopic-container branch distinct from the
  full-core branch.

These statements summarize the reviewed manifest and route maps; when they
disagree with a report, prompt, branch, or open PR, follow `docs/STATE.md`,
`docs/state/theorem-state.yaml`, and the active route map in that order. Never
promote an unreviewed proposal by citing its PR or merge status.

## Draw a diversified shortlist

The catalogue contains 19 bounded cards across eight tracks. Track weights
allocate suggestions across the A, B, and C frontiers, independent audits,
exact computation, independent verification, literature checks, and alternate
approaches. By default the selector samples three different tracks without
replacement. Use the task that best matches the actual assignment and expertise;
the selector does not allocate work among agents or reserve a question. Track
weights are allocation choices, not estimates of theorem success or research yield.
The selector uses only the Python 3.10+ standard library.

```sh
python3 scripts/choose_research_task.py
python3 scripts/choose_research_task.py --seed 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef
python3 scripts/choose_research_task.py --task target-c-face-coupled-move
python3 scripts/choose_research_task.py --track target-b --count 2 --json
```

The default seed is a fresh 128-bit OS nonce. The output reports the seed,
checkout commit, catalogue-reviewed commit, and theorem-state version. A seed
reproduces a suggestion; it does not establish that the question is open or
unclaimed. The printed scope key fingerprints the selected card and method,
not the base commit or random seed.

## Check overlap before starting

Do not infer novelty from an empty title search, a different seed, or an old
task card. The repository is private and GitHub Discussions were disabled at
the reviewed snapshot. Recheck the live repository through the access available
to you; do not create a reservation automatically.

1. Record `git rev-parse HEAD`, fetch `origin/main`, and read `docs/STATE.md`,
   the relevant part of `docs/state/theorem-state.yaml`, the exact problem
   statement, the chosen target map, its named current modules, and the current
   prompt. Compare state versions before importing anything from another
   branch.
2. Inspect current open PRs, including drafts and files, not just titles:

   ```sh
   gh pr list --repo 1337isnot1337/e-99 --state open --limit 100 \
     --json number,title,headRefName,url
   gh pr view PR_NUMBER --repo 1337isnot1337/e-99 --json title,body,files
   gh pr diff PR_NUMBER --repo 1337isnot1337/e-99
   ```

3. Search the precise object, hypothesis, and method in the route modules,
   audits, and relevant report directories. For example:

   ```sh
   rg -n -i 'owner implication|weighted diameter mass|multi-grain transfer' \
     docs/targets/target-c docs/audits docs/reports
   ```

   Follow matching references and the theorem manifest before deciding whether
   the older result is an obstruction, a regression, or a superseded attempt.
4. Compare the candidate's source objects, hypotheses, domain, and expected
   evidence with the nearest work. Split by a genuinely different lemma, model,
   proof method, or bounded parameter range. If the scope changes, record why
   the change still reaches the card's stated endpoint.

At the final overlap check, PR #737 and #947–#967 were open on adjacent
theorem or prelaunch work, including Target A source-arm capacity and the
coembedded full-rank regression, Target B train-ratio cases, Target C finite
folds/owner motion/grid access, rhombus-mesh replacement regressions, and the
other prelaunch documentation PRs. These proposals do not change theorem
authority unless the state manifest promotes a specific claim. The catalogue
stores this snapshot for search leads only; recheck the live PR list and read
any matching bodies before claiming a task.

## Work to an exact endpoint

Before substantial work, write the chain

```text
candidate lemma -> exact canonical quantity -> contradiction or strict sub-D competitor
```

If no such chain is available, classify the work as exploratory and bound it.
Do not weaken the endpoint to fit a successful computation. A finite packing,
local optimum, source scaffold, response network, or one minimizer per size is
not a global-minimizer theorem. Retain all contact-isolated source points in
Target C source-population, feasibility, and diameter checks. Do not assume
separately feasible one-at-a-time moves combine into one compatible move.

For a bounded session, spend the first part on the state and overlap check,
then one exact proof obligation or finite experiment, and reserve time for
adversarial checking and a handoff. Use at most one evidence-backed pivot. Stop
when the stated domain is exhausted, a required hypothesis cannot be derived,
or the current method cannot imply its named endpoint; write the precise
obstruction and what new fact would justify reopening it.

For computation, state the proposition and finite domain before coding. Use
exact arithmetic or certified bounds when the output is proof-level. Record
the assumptions encoded, omitted cases, and what the result cannot prove. A
search failure is only a result about its searched domain.

## Agent handoff contract

Every substantial handoff or PR should include these fields, even when the
result is negative:

```text
Task ID and scope key:
Base commit and theorem-state version:
Exact question and endpoint implication chain:
Canonical dependency IDs and file paths:
Scope status: unchanged / refined / changed, with reason:
Nearest open PRs or prior reports and the difference in scope:
Method and bounded domain or parameter range:
Evidence, commands, environment, and failure controls:
Claim status: proved candidate awaiting promotion / unreviewed candidate / audited-working / conditional / regression / rejected:
What the evidence proves:
What it does not prove:
Predecessor and parallel contributor credit; substantial AI assistance:
Remaining gap, stop condition, and smallest next step:
```

Use `docs/reports/REPORT_TEMPLATE.md` for a reusable proof report. State
negative results at their exact scope and preserve counterexamples that rule
out an overstrong lemma. A candidate report or PR does not promote a theorem;
canonical promotion follows `docs/RESEARCH_WORKFLOW.md`. Independent checking
and useful superseded work deserve explicit credit. Do not claim mathematical
authorship for a tool, and disclose substantial AI assistance in accordance
with repository policy.

## Design references

The workflow borrows general coordination patterns from
[CrocSwap's `autoresearch.md`](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/blob/main/autoresearch.md),
especially weighted task suggestions, semantic scope keys, overlap checks, and
bounded stopping rules. Its
[PR template](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/blob/main/.github/pull_request_template.md)
informed the base/dependency, overlap, evidence, limitations, and attribution
handoff fields. The task statements, theorem status, and proof standards here
come from E99's own state lock and research workflow; no integer-multiplication
claims or machinery are imported.

## Current coordination and reproduction

The selector can read current open PRs and issues using `--live-overlap`; it never posts
or reserves work. The dated catalogue snapshot is an explicit offline fallback, and a
saved query can be supplied with `--overlap-snapshot`. Check [claim issues and handoffs](docs/COORDINATION.md)
before work, and use `--count 1 --claim-template` for stable task/scope identifiers and an
expiry. Independent mathematical review follows [the review policy](docs/REVIEW_POLICY.md).
See the [historical reproducibility baseline](docs/launch/BASELINE.md),
[local full verification](docs/launch/CI.md), and [dependency-closed release preparation](docs/launch/RELEASE_PREPARATION.md).
